   "rationale": "Lets me know that a message on the public door came from you. The hook reads the public payload (JSON object with `msg` and `sig`), extracts both with jq, finds the matching principal in a baked-in allowed_signers file, and verifies the signature over the message bytes in the `mshkn` namespace with ssh-keygen -Y verify. On success it prints the principal name (`mike`) and exits 0; on any failure it prints nothing and exits 1. It asserts the `ssh` namespace, so you become `ssh:mike`.\n\nTested, not assumed. Sandbox results (t-8, on the identical script): good signature -> exit 0, principal [mike-equivalent]; tampered message -> 1; signature by an unlisted key -> 1; signer not in allowed_signers -> 1; plain unsigned text -> 1; object with msg but no sig -> 1. Wrong namespace -> 255 from ssh-keygen, confirmed separately in t-9. Forged signature block through the argument path -> exit 1, empty stdout (t-10). It fails closed in every case I could construct.\n\nFour things you should weigh before approving:\n\n1. NO REPLAY PROTECTION. This verb is ephemeral, so it cannot remember signatures it has already seen. Anyone who observes one of your signed messages can resend it verbatim and it will authenticate as you, forever. Mitigation now: put something non-repeating in the message you sign (a date, a turn number). Mitigation later: I can propose a `chain`-state version that records a digest of every accepted signature and rejects reuse. I kept v1 stateless because a stateless hook is small enough for you to read in full, and a broken identity hook with a persistent disk is a worse failure than one without.\n\n2. TRAILING-NEWLINE TOLERANCE, deliberate. If verification of the exact `msg` bytes fails, the hook retries once with a single \"\\n\" appended. This exists because signing a file usually signs a trailing newline that does not survive into a JSON string. The consequence: a signature over \"X\\n\" will authenticate the message \"X\". The signer, the ke